WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.496
Back in the warehouse, the floor keeps filling, and the cleaning crew isn't to blame.

2
00:00:07.496 --> 00:00:14.041
Somewhere, a list pinned to the noticeboard keeps growing, and every shelf number on it is a box the crew must keep.

3
00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:18.316
Earlier in this lesson, we counted the boxes.

4
00:00:18.316 --> 00:00:29.408
After 20,000 uploads to the thumbnail service, the class histogram's top rows said: 43 MB of byte arrays, and exactly 20,000 CachedThumbnail objects.

5
00:00:29.408 --> 00:00:32.757
That's 20,000 more than anyone meant to keep.

6
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:36.484
Which brings up a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:36.484 --> 00:00:38.210
Byte arrays are everywhere.

8
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.321
So who is actually holding these?

9
00:00:40.321 --> 00:00:42.879
The histogram says what fills the floor.

10
00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:44.541
It can't say who holds it.

11
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.190
For that, we take a photo of every shelf, for a detective.

12
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:56.701
That's a heap dump: a file with every object on the heap, and every reference between them, in a format called HPROF.

13
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:00.175
There are three ways to take one.

14
00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:03.103
From a running JVM, jcmd GC.heap_dump.

15
00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:11.037
Its own help text says Impact: High, because it runs a full GC first and stops the JVM while it writes.

16
00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:18.201
Or the flag HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError, so the JVM writes one the moment it runs out of heap.

17
00:01:18.201 --> 00:01:24.440
Or from code, with HotSpotDiagnosticMXBean, which is what the lesson's demo does.

18
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.798
Here's the graph inside this photo.

19
00:01:27.798 --> 00:01:32.243
The noticeboard, a static field named THUMBNAILS, holds the map.

20
00:01:32.243 --> 00:01:33.772
The map holds a table.

21
00:01:33.772 --> 00:01:35.786
The table holds 20,000 nodes.

22
00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:40.440
And each node holds a key, and a thumbnail with its 2 KB of pixels.

23
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:46.626
There's a second notice on the board: FORMATS, the list of picture formats the site accepts.

24
00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:50.707
Every thumbnail points at one of its strings: image slash jpeg.

25
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:52.028
Now, two sizes.

26
00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:57.429
The shallow size of an object is its own bytes: the header and its fields.

27
00:01:57.429 --> 00:01:59.764
The map itself is only 64 bytes.

28
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:04.982
The retained size is everything that would be freed if that one object were gone.

29
00:02:04.982 --> 00:02:07.953
So picture tearing the map's note off the board.

30
00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:09.501
Which boxes fall with it?

31
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:11.791
The table and the nodes, sure.

32
00:02:11.791 --> 00:02:14.330
The keys, the thumbnails, the pixels.

33
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.828
But what about the shared string, image slash jpeg?

34
00:02:17.828 --> 00:02:18.994
Does it fall too?

35
00:02:18.994 --> 00:02:20.092
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:23.364
No.

37
00:02:23.364 --> 00:02:28.168
The FORMATS list still reaches it, so it isn't in the map's retained set.

38
00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:31.656
Only what the map alone keeps reachable goes with it.

39
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:34.015
Now add it up.

40
00:02:34.015 --> 00:02:37.636
20,000 pixel arrays at 2,064 bytes.

41
00:02:37.636 --> 00:02:42.395
Keys at 80, thumbnails at 32, and nodes at 32.

42
00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.672
Plus the 128 KB table.

43
00:02:44.672 --> 00:02:46.637
That's about 44 MB.

44
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.200
About 44 MB, held by a 64-byte object.

45
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.220
The smallest thing in the photo, holding the biggest pile.

46
00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.161
That's one strong paperclip.

47
00:02:56.161 --> 00:03:01.289
So the point is: shallow size tells you nothing about who holds the floor.

48
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.283
Is the arithmetic right?

49
00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.887
Let's not take my word for it.

50
00:03:06.887 --> 00:03:14.178
The demo measures the live heap after a full GC, clears the map, and measures again.

51
00:03:14.178 --> 00:03:17.477
The cache kept 44,496,872 bytes alive.

52
00:03:17.477 --> 00:03:23.119
That's 44.5 MB, the sum we added up, give or take a little noise.

53
00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:24.882
Now the names.

54
00:03:24.882 --> 00:03:30.340
The map dominates the pixels, because every path from a root to them goes through the map.

55
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.464
The shared string has a second path, so the map doesn't dominate it.

56
00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:39.622
MAT's dominator tree lists objects by retained size, biggest first.

57
00:03:39.622 --> 00:03:47.942
For this dump, the map should sit at the top with about 44 MB, and its path to a GC root should end at the static field THUMBNAILS.

58
00:03:47.942 --> 00:03:54.928
The lesson couldn't run MAT, so that's a prediction from the definition and the measurement, not a screenshot.

59
00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:58.002
So, a histogram says what fills the floor.

60
00:03:58.002 --> 00:04:00.529
The dominator tree says who holds it.

61
00:04:00.529 --> 00:04:01.553
Fix the holder.

62
00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:05.481
And here's the holder, in Java and in Kotlin.

63
00:04:05.481 --> 00:04:08.226
A static map from upload id to thumbnail.

64
00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:12.044
Every upload adds an entry, and nothing ever removes one.

65
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:14.843
Here the photo picture breaks.

66
00:04:14.843 --> 00:04:20.346
A photo shows one moment, while a leak is a habit: the code adds a note on every upload.

67
00:04:20.346 --> 00:04:25.349
You find the list in the dump, but you fix it in the code that keeps writing it.

68
00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:28.225
Decide a maximum, and make the map enforce it.

69
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:30.045
Two cautions.

70
00:04:30.045 --> 00:04:33.203
A heap dump is big: this one was about 52 MB.

71
00:04:33.203 --> 00:04:35.588
And it only photographs the floor.

72
00:04:35.588 --> 00:04:40.851
Memory outside the heap, in the yard out back, isn't in the picture at all.

73
00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:44.040
So, who is holding the byte arrays?

74
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.014
Take the photo.

75
00:04:45.014 --> 00:04:48.522
Tear off notes in your head, and ask which boxes fall.

76
00:04:48.522 --> 00:04:53.783
The object with the biggest pile, and its path to a root, names the field to fix.

77
00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:55.513
Quick check.

78
00:04:55.513 --> 00:05:01.071
Which tool names the field THUMBNAILS: the class histogram, or the dominator tree?

79
00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.576
The dominator tree, through the path to a GC root.

80
00:05:07.576 --> 00:05:09.827
The histogram only counts classes.
